#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134706} :activity/id #uuid "8d26a5ff-3bd1-560e-af9c-1dfbf5779d4c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050897 :topic/id #uuid "6f612f55-1cd5-5cf2-9d34-1952de92e150" :topic/math-academy-id 2170} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134706 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135063 :tutorial/content "We've seen how to construct a simple linear regression model given some bivariate data $(x,y).$ We can generalize this technique to fit data to an arbitrary polynomial. We call this process **polynomial regression**.\n\nThe following table gives the number of units $x$ (in hundreds) produced by a small company and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) earned for producing $x$ units:\n\n| Goods $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Profit $(y)$ | $16$ | $11$ | $15$ | $18$ |\n\nSuppose we want to find the quadratic function\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points.\n\n![](images/93/93fe1784b89ce4c892c84a03a0dc80909f16f46ac463c5cc30b40f869a7802bf.png)\n\nSubstituting the values of $x$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+1{\\beta }_{1}+{1}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=16 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+2{\\beta }_{1}+{2}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=11 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+3{\\beta }_{1}+{3}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=15 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+4{\\beta }_{1}+{4}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system can be written using matrix notation as\n\n![](images/72/729e20ddab6e49b109ebccbb049b1abb9ca2fc11003a9e7f4828bff305a283b4.svg)\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nSince our points do not lie on a quadratic curve, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0, \\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0, \\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the curve that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta }=[\\begin{aligned}22.5 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the quadratic curve of best fit (regression curve) is \n\n$$\ny = 22.5 - 9x + 2x^2.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a9d76d67-952b-5a5c-8891-757974db3a7f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3654 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5282b6c3-a052-5524-8cfc-ee4738c1759c" :step/math-academy-id 20311 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134708}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134707 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133806 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133615 :question/id #uuid "98ec57bb-04f5-559a-9057-854de19eda7f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5612"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b2c90d8-0f2e-5f84-bf67-7620d6f1d308" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400819} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551244}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Design Matrix When the Regression Curve Contains Zero Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "3d8abdce-a161-520c-ae2e-5c81f7417b17" :step/math-academy-id 20312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134708 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133807 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133616 :question/id #uuid "f5354fe5-fa4b-5443-a097-40174b0b7b16" :question/math-academy-id "e-5613"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0f92926-0c15-554a-8fba-55b05d435980" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050179}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558567}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Expression for a Least-Squares Regression Curve"} :step/id #uuid "8359737f-d4ca-50ac-871a-c58906d6165e" :step/math-academy-id 20313 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134734}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134734 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133823 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133635 :question/id #uuid "75c0086b-db74-5f01-8d66-f023dd7b575d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6094"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa5bf185-de29-5185-ba57-c64250332dfa" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371817} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558492} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558545}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Elements of a Polynomial Regression Model"} :step/id #uuid "91936449-d1f1-5123-8737-4025e640187f" :step/math-academy-id 22549 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134890}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134890 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135144 :tutorial/content "In some cases, we might need to constrain the regression curve so that some of its coefficients equal zero. \n\nConsider the table below, which gives the number of units $x$ (in hundreds) produced by a small company and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) earned for producing $x$ units. \n\n| Goods $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Profit $(y)$ | $2$ | $5$ | $8$ | $18$ |\n\nIt's known that the profit equals zero when the company does not produce any goods, so $y=0$ when $x=0.$ The company wishes to find a *quadratic* curve that best fits the data *subject to* the constraint that it should pass through the point $(0,0).$\n\nSo, our regression curve must pass through the point $(0,0).$ In other words, the $y$-intercept of the quadratic curve must be $0.$ Therefore, we want to find the quadratic function\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points.\n\n![](images/0f/0f98711850478ba6a152103452446e1b2a97ca64c2d47ab7c257db2d05335fcf.png)\n\nSubstituting the values of $x$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1}+{1}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=2 \\\\ 2{\\beta }_{1}+{2}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=5 \\\\ 3{\\beta }_{1}+{3}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=8 \\\\ 4{\\beta }_{1}+{4}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system can be written using matrix notation as\n\n![](images/ed/ed2c245e39c03d6d60500866bba6daf37c4e86becb570720b3c6e65aad90720e.svg)\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nThe rest of the arguments are the same as before. Since our points do not lie on a quadratic curve, there will be no parameters $\\beta_1$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_1$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution." :tutorial/id #uuid "34af7736-1458-54b5-b831-80b4e22a03b6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6152 :tutorial/title "Regression Curves Containing Zero Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "e556ba45-b685-5183-8ad9-099c86720b9b" :step/math-academy-id 37335 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134707}}] :activity/title "Polynomial Regression With Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134030}]}
